<DocsLayout title="Software Factory" toc={toc}>
    <h1 id="why">Design the part and the bot that builds it<span class="title-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h1>
    <p>
        A mechanical engineer specifies a part&rsquo;s shape, fit, and
        tolerances. A machine manufactures it without the engineer guiding
        every movement. ChunkHound and pi-schematic give you the tools to
        apply that model to software: design the <strong>digital part</strong>
        and the <strong>automation bot that builds it</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The part has behavior, interfaces, and acceptance tests. The bot has
        a procedure: investigate, build, test, review, and stop for human
        judgment when needed. Models can misunderstand instructions, so
        checks matter. Engineers retain ownership of the design and what ships.
    </p>
    <p>
        Designing the bot means encoding engineering expertise as code in a
        tailored, deterministic control plane. It places active guardrails
        where they matter: before edits, after checks, and when a run must
        stop. Models supply judgment; your code governs what happens next,
        including model, cost, and context policies.
    </p>

    <p>
        ChunkHound supplies the evidence; pi-schematic controls the procedure.
        Instead of constantly pressing the agent&rsquo;s buttons, you define
        when the bot must turn to you. It runs the process between those
        points, freeing your attention for decisions that need your judgment
        and for improving the process itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="start">Installation</h2>
    <p>
        Install pi-schematic in Pi from its repository. The package is not on
        npm yet.
    </p>
    <DocCode lang="bash" code={`pi install git:github.com/chunkhound/pi-schematic`} />
    <p>
        To run from a checkout instead, run
        <code>pi install ./pi-schematic</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        To add ChunkHound, follow <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting Started</a>
        to index a repository and configure your MCP client.
    </p>

    <h2 id="schematic">The building blocks</h2>

    <SoftwareFactoryDiagram />

    <p>
        These controls work together, from preventing accidental edits to
        running a complete automation bot.
    </p>
    <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>
            <strong>Posture</strong> &mdash; prevent accidental changes.
            Grounding, planning, and review need access to the repository
            without changing it. Readonly mode guards against accidental
            writes. It is not a security boundary; see the
            <a href="https://github.com/chunkhound/pi-schematic/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#behavioral-notes">design and limits</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Memory</strong> &mdash; keep what matters, not the whole
            conversation. Long conversations bury important details; lasting
            memories can become outdated. The agent keeps a short-lived
            notebook of findings and decisions as it works, rather than relying
            on a summary made when the context fills. A
            <code>/handoff &lt;prompt&gt;</code> starts a fresh context with the
            notebook and the next instruction. The agent can also hand off
            when the topic changes. <code>/new</code> clears the notebook for
            a new task.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Delegation</strong> &mdash; divide the work and choose the model.
            An orchestrator needs to break a job into focused tasks without
            doing every investigation itself. The <code>spawn</code> tool
            sends those tasks to sub-agents, each with its own context. Model
            Groups handle a separate decision: which model should do each
            task. Stable group names let you change the models behind them
            without rewriting the instructions that delegate the work.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Configuration</strong> &mdash; activate the right harness settings
            with the task. Let the harness handle the setup so you can focus
            on the work, not the settings. Frontmatter declares readonly mode,
            a Model Group or specific model, and thinking level. The harness
            applies them automatically each time you invoke the skill or
            command, so the task starts with the intended setup.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Automation</strong> &mdash; turn the process into a bot.
            Workflows are where these controls become a tailored automation
            bot. You write a deterministic control plane for the process:
            what runs, which results permit the next step, and where human
            judgment is required. Workflows can control the whole harness,
            not just send prompts. You improve that process over time,
            freeing people to concentrate where their judgment matters
            instead of continually directing the agent.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Ready to build your first software factory?
        Start with <a href="/docs/workflows/">workflows &rarr;</a>.
    </p>
</DocsLayout>

